WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:04.520
Considering all the fame and fortune and fans
who followed his work, it came as somewhat

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:09.210
of a shock when Joe Pesci announced in 1999
that he was retiring from acting to pursue

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:10.320
a music career.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:14.300
Twenty years later, Pesci has come out of
retirement to act again, and we know the real

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:15.320
reason why.

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:19.600
Pesci is reuniting with several of his longtime
collaborators for his first major film role

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:23.440
post-retirement in a movie that's already
generating Oscar buzz.

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:28.220
In 2017, Deadline reported that Pesci would
re-team with his Goodfellas director Martin

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:33.260
Scorsese for The Irishman, which also stars
Pesci's frequent co-star Robert De Niro.

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:38.120
A biographical crime drama based on the book
I Heard You Paint Houses by Charles Brandt,

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:43.400
The Irishman focuses on the famous labor union
leader and alleged mob hitman Frank "The Irishman"

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:45.240
Sheeran, played in the film by De Niro.

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:49.940
Pesci is set to play American mafioso Russell
Bufalino, the leader of the Bufalino crime

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:52.640
family to whom Sheeran was believed to have
connections.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:57.040
Following Sheeran in his old age as he reflects
on the crimes that defined his career in the

00:00:57.050 --> 00:01:02.450
Mafia, The Irishman also stars Al Pacino as
Jimmy Hoffa, a fellow union leader and President

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:07.660
of the International Brotherhood of Teamsters
who disappeared in 1975 and was declared legally

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:09.280
dead in 1982.

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:13.560
Before his death in 2003, Sheeran claimed
that he was the man who killed his old friend

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:14.400
Hoffa.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:18.680
After Paramount Pictures dropped the project,
Netflix acquired distribution rights to The

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:22.700
Irishman, which is scheduled to hit the streaming
service in late 2019.

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:27.000
That's around the time that The Irishman will
also see a theatrical launch to meet the requirements

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:28.800
for Academy Awards eligibility.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:32.540
Considering the talent involved, one could
argue that coming out of retirement to star

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:34.920
in The Irishman was a no-brainer for Pesci.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:39.320
Reuniting with Scorsese, De Niro, and Pacino
for a movie that could very well earn several

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:40.500
Academy Awards?

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:43.820
Pesci would be foolish to turn that down to
stay out of the public eye.

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:47.680
But that's actually what happened, at least
according to past reports.

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:52.140
According to Deadline, Pesci didn't immediately
jump on the chance to step back into the limelight

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:53.160
for The Irishman.

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:56.960
Pesci is said to have repeatedly turned down
the offer, with some sources stating that

00:01:56.970 --> 00:01:59.270
he said no about 50 times.

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:03.150
This stubbornness could have been a combination
of a reluctance to come out of retirement

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:07.020
and a past bad experience with getting lined
up to play a mobster in a movie.

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:11.540
Pesci was reportedly promised the role of
real-life Gambino crime family capo Angelo

00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:16.420
Ruggiero in director Kevin Connolly's Gotti,
and put on 30 pounds for the part, which he

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:18.020
never actually landed.

00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:23.100
He wound up suing the producers for $3 million,
and settled out of court in 2013.

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:24.440
And if you think that's funny…

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:26.080
"I'm funny, how?

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:27.920
Like I'm a clown, I amuse you?

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:29.530
I make you laugh?

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:30.980
I'm here to f---in' amuse you?

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:31.980
What do you mean 'funny'?

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:32.700
Funny how?

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:33.820
How am I funny?"

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:38.520
It evidently took persistence and convincing
to get Pesci to sign on for The Irishman and

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:42.560
officially come out of retirement to act again,
but it sounds like the tipping point came

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:46.800
when Pesci got the full scope of the project
and the opportunities it could afford him.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:50.900
Knowing he would reunite with Scorsese was
one thing, and learning that he'd be joined

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:52.640
by a glittering cast was another.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:57.250
Still, we suspect that when Pesci got a taste
of the intense true-life story, he realized

00:02:57.250 --> 00:03:00.520
how good The Irishman would be and ultimately
said yes.

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:04.540
It was always going to take a fantastic gig
to bring Pesci out of retirement, and the

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:07.820
role of Russell Bufalino in The Irishman seems
to be a winner.

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:12.940
See Pesci make his triumphant return to acting
when The Irishman rolls out in late 2019.

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:14.060
"I nailed it.

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:14.580
I did.

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:15.340
I nailed it."

